-
**Remove.**
Click
a
connection,
or
focus
it
and
press
Enter
or
Space,
to
select
it.
Then
press
Delete
or
Backspace
with
focus
on
the
canvas,
or
use
**Remove
connection**
in
the
selected
connection's
toolbar
beside
**Reset
route**:
it
is
32
px
and
44
px
under
a
coarse
pointer,
is
disabled
with
the
voice-step
message
on
a
protected
edge,
and
moves
focus
to
**Undo**
afterwards.
One
connection
is
removed
at
a
time;
a
selected
node
is
never
removed
by
an
edge's
removal.
After
a
mouse
pan
the
toolbar
briefly
refuses:
d3-zoom
ends
a
pan
that
moved
by
adding
a
window-capture
`click.drag`
listener
(d3-drag's
`yesdrag`)
that
cancels
the
next
click
anywhere
on
the
page,
and
removes
it
in
a
zero-delay
task,
so
a
Remove
connection
click
in
between
was
swallowed
while
the
button
looked
available.
A
per-canvas
gate
(`editor/viewport-gesture.ts`)
is
`panning`
from
the
canvas's
first
`onMove`
with
a
`mousemove`
source
event,
`settling`
from
the
next
mouse
release,
and
ready
two
tasks
later.
Its
single
passive
window-capture
`mouseup`
listener
is
added
when
the
canvas
mounts,
so
it
sees
each
release
before
d3-zoom's
per-press
listener
stops
it;
it
queues
a
zero-delay
task
that
queues
a
second,
and
only
the
second,
which
runs
after
d3's
cleanup,
makes
it
ready,
and
only
if
no
mouse
move
came
since
and
the
canvas
is
still
mounted.
Meanwhile
**Remove
connection**
and
**Reset
route**
are
`aria-disabled`
(dimmed,
keeping
focus,
their
hit
target
and
`nopan`)
with
`data-gesture`
set
to
the
state,
and
their
handlers
refuse
on
that
same
rendered
state:
a
click,
Enter
or
Space
then
does
nothing
and
says
nothing,
and
the
first
click
once
they
show
as
available
acts
once.
The
pan's
own
click
is
still
cancelled
and
the
selection
kept.
Wheel,
touch,
Fit
View
and
other
programmatic
moves
add
no
blocker
and
never
touch
the
gate;
they
also
cannot
strand
it,
since
it
does
not
wait
for
xyflow's
`onMoveEnd`,
which
a
later
wheel
or
programmatic
end
replaces.
A
release
that
never
reaches
the
page
leaves
the
gate
unavailable
until
the
next
mouse
release
there,
which
also
ends
d3's
gesture.
Nothing
is
replayed,
prevented
or
patched;
a
protected
voice
step
keeps
its
own
disabled
reason.
The
Space
cards
offer's
buttons
(#208,
"Space
cards"
above)
follow
this
same
gate.
Clicks
on
xyflow's
zoom
controls
and
after
a
node
drag
meet
the
same
d3
blocker
and
are
not
gated.
The
screen-reader
description
of
a
focused
connection
says
"Press
Enter
or
Space
to
select
the
connection,
then
Delete
or
Backspace
to
remove
it;
its
toolbar
also
has
Remove
connection.
To
connect
its
output
elsewhere,
remove
the
connection
and
draw
from
the
output
again."
